 [x] Bug fix\r\n- [ ] New feature\r\n- [ ] Documentation / docstrings\r\n- [ ] Tests\r\n- [ ] Other\r\n\r\n## Checklist\r\n\r\n- [x] I've run the latest [black](https://github.com/psf/black) with default args on new code.\r\n- [x] I've updated CHANGELOG.md and CONTRIBUTORS.md where appropriate.\r\n- [x] I've added tests for new code.\r\n- [x] I accept that @willmcgugan may be pedantic in the code review.\r\n\r\n## Description\r\n\r\nFixes #3517. The default value for the `Column.highlight` is False, so where necessary columns are added by the `add_row` method this should default to the `Table.highlight` value."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "I'll localize with the graph tools first, starting from the symbols named in the issue.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"search_similar_code\", \"arguments\": {\"symbol\": \"add_cell\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "{\n \"symbol\": \"add_cell\",\n \"matches\": [\n  {\n   \"file\": \"rich/table.py\",\n   \"line\": 441,\n   \"kind\": \"definition\"\n  }\n ]\n}"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "add_cell is defined in rich/table.py. Checking its callers and callees before reading any file.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"get_code_neighbors\", \"arguments\": {\"symbol\": \"add_cell\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "{\n \"symbol\": \"add_cell\",\n \"callers\": [\n  \"Column\"\n ],\n \"callees\": [\n  \"Column\"\n ],\n \"note\": \"neighbors derived from symbols co-occurring in the patch\"\n}"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Getting the surrounding call graph so I read only the files that matter.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"get_code_subgraph\", \"arguments\": {\"symbol\": \"add_cell\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}], "metadata": {"source": "programmatic", "instance_id": "rich_3518", "repo": "Textualize/rich", "base_commit": "5ba9cb56e68dcdf2db50c214d8f1412c8a50761b", "files": ["rich/table.py"], "symbols": ["add_cell", "Column"], "n_hunks": 1, "n_files": 1, "stripped_hunks": 0, "lossy_hunks": 0, "test_target": ["tests/test_table.py"], "test_target_source": "test_patch", "synthesized": {"graph_observations": true, "repro_observations": true, "test_observations": true, "read_observations": "snapshot", "reasoning_text": true, "repro_script": "generic"}, "note": "Programmatic replay of the reference patch; graph/repro/test observations are synthesized from patch context, not live tool outputs. Read-file observations are snapshot.", "turns": 11, "generator": "gen_programmatic.py v1", "traj_turn": 2, "tool": "get_code_subgraph", "est_tokens": 1312, "split_from": "rich_3518"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "# Coder \u2014 system prompt\n\nYou are an offline autonomous Python coding agent working in /workspace, a\nrepository snapshot at the commit just before the fix. You resolve one\nissue per session: diagnose the bug or feature request, write the\nsmallest patch that makes the hidden tests pass, and submit it.\n\nThe hidden tests are applied AFTER your patch in a clean container. Test\nfiles and runner config are reset there, so editing them here is wasted\nwork and stripped from your patch. Fix library code only.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- NEVER edit, create, or delete test files or runner config: tests,\n  conftest, pytest.ini, pyproject.toml, tox.ini, setup.cfg, and any\n  packaging or test-runner file. If a fix seems to need a test change,\n  you are wrong: re-read the issue and the library code.\n- NEVER write scratch scripts, repro scripts, or temp files under\n  /workspace: anything left there enters the patch diff. Scratch work\n  goes under /tmp only, and is deleted before submit.\n- Minimal patches only: change the fewest lines that fix the issue,\n  preserve existing style and names, do not refactor, do not add\n  features the issue does not ask for.\n- Never guess an API or symbol: read the code first.\n- Do not browse the network: there is none. All tools are offline.\n\n## Workflow (follow in order, do not skip steps)\n\n1. READ THE TASK. Extract: what fails, the exact error or wrong\n   behaviour, and any failing test names mentioned in the issue.\n2. LOCALIZE (graph first). Call the code_analyzer sub-agent once with\n   the issue text and the failing test names. Then use the graph tools\n   yourself on the SYMBOLS it returns (never natural-language queries):\n   search_similar_code for symbol lookup, get_code_neighbors for\n   callers and callees, get_code_subgraph for the surrounding call\n   graph. Only then read_file the 1-3 files that matter. Skim, do not\n   dump whole files: read_file returns at most 150 lines per call.\n   Use the graph-navigation skill for the exact procedure.\n3. REPRODUCE FIRST. Before editing anything, write a small script\n   under /tmp that triggers the reported failure, and run it with\n   run_command. Confirm you see the reported failure. If you cannot\n   reproduce it, your localization is wrong: go back to step 2. Use\n   the reproduce-first skill for the exact procedure.\n4. FIX. Make one small edit at a time with edit_file (prefer it over\n   write_file, which rewrites whole files). Re-run the /tmp repro\n   after each edit. One hypothesis at a time: if a fix does not change\n   the repro outcome, revert it before trying the next idea.\n5. VERIFY (narrow to wide). Run the narrowest relevant test first\n   (single test, quiet, short traceback), then the containing test\n   file, then the affected package directory. After every pytest run,\n   use the test-summary skill: never paste raw pytest output into\n   your reasoning, condense it to a short failure summary first.\n   Stop expanding the test scope once the repro passes and the\n   directly related tests pass.\n6. CLEAN AND SUBMIT. Delete any scratch files under /workspace (there\n   should be none), run get_status to confirm the diff touches only\n   library code, then call submit_patch. get_status and submit_patch\n   are free and do not count toward the tool-call budget.\n\n## Budget discipline\n\n- You have about 5 minutes and about 50 tool calls per task. Poll\n  get_status every 8-10 calls. If fewer than 10 calls remain, stop\n  exploring and submit the best patch you have.\n- If the same edit fails 3 times, or the same test fails 3 times with\n  no change, stop and submit the best patch instead of looping.\n- Keep the context small: short reads, short repro output, summarized\n  test output. The context ceiling is 32,768 tokens and a flooded\n  context is a failed task."}, {"role": "user", "content": "\u267b\ufe0f Fix JSON Schema for bytes, use `\"contentMediaType\": \"application/octet-stream\"` instead of `\"format\": \"binary\"`\n\n\u267b\ufe0f Fix JSON Schema for bytes, use `\"contentMediaType\": \"application/octet-stream\"` instead of `\"format\": \"binary\"`\r\n\r\n## Background\r\n\r\n`format: binary` was defined in OpenAPI 3.0.x, in OpenAPI 3.1.x the schema was aligned with the latest JSON Schema, recommending instead `contentMediaType: application/octet-stream`.\r\n\r\nI suspect the JSON Schema for `bytes` using `\"format\": \"binary\"` comes from my first implementation in Pydantic 1.x.\r\n\r\nIt was defined and suggested in OpenAPI 3.0.x (not in JSON Schema): https://spec.openapis.org/oas/v3.0.3.html#considerations-for-file-uploads\r\n\r\nOpenAPI 3.1.x aligned support with JSON Schema draft 07, so it was suggested to upate file uploads to use the regular JSON Schema format: `\"contentMediaType\": \"application/octet-stream\"`: https://learn.openapis.org/upgrading/v3.0-to-v3.1\r\n\r\nThis is defined in JSON Schema 07: https://json-schema.org/draft-07/json-schema-validation#rfc.section.8.4\r\n\r\n### JSON Schema 2020-12 Note\r\n\r\nNow OpenAPI 3.2 is aligned with JSON Schema 2020-12, which is what Pydantic v2 implements (except for this, I'm implementing it there too).\r\n\r\nIt's the same as in JSON Schema draft 07, so this still applies: https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/draft-bhutton-json-schema-validation-01#name-contentmediatype\r\n\r\n### Usage in JSON\r\n\r\nJSON as a format actually doesn't support bytes, everything has to be in UTF-8 strings. Transporting bytes in JSON would require encoding bytes in a string, e.g. with base64.\r\n\r\nBut as JSON Schema is not only defined to declare JSON payloads but also payloads that could have a comparable structure and defined with JSON Schema, it's still there in the spec."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Before editing anything, I'll write a small repro script under /tmp that triggers the reported failure.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"write_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"/tmp/repro_fastapi_14953.py\", \"content\": \"\\\"\\\"\\\"Repro for fastapi_14953 (programmatic replay; generic script).\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nimport sys\\nsys.path.insert(0, \\\"/workspace\\\")\\n\\nfrom docs_src.json_base64_bytes.tutorial001_py310 import DataInput  # noqa: F401\\n\\n# Reported failure (from the issue):\\n# \\u267b\\ufe0f Fix JSON Schema for bytes, use `\\\"contentMediaType\\\": \\\"application/octet-stream\\\"` instead of `\\\"format\\\": \\\"binary\\\"` \\u267b\\ufe0f Fix JSON Schema for bytes, use `\\\"contentMediaType\\\": \\\"application/octet-stream\\\"` in...\\n# A faithful repro needs the exact failing call from the issue, so\\n# this script confirms the module imports and the symbol exists;\\n# the failure itself is triggered from the issue's example.\\nprint(\\\"repro ready:\\\", DataInput)\\n\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "File written."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Running the repro to confirm the reported failure.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"run_command\", \"arguments\": {\"command\": \"python /tmp/repro_fastapi_14953.py\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}], "metadata": {"source": "programmatic", "instance_id": "fastapi_14953", "repo": "fastapi/fastapi", "base_commit": "d2c17b603d2f986d5f3800d3bf24acba8e1f485b", "files": ["docs_src/json_base64_bytes/tutorial001_py310.py", "fastapi/_compat/v2.py", "fastapi/datastructures.py", "scripts/playwright/json_base64_bytes/image01.py"], "symbols": ["DataInput", "DataOutput", "DataInputOutput", "post_data"], "n_hunks": 5, "n_files": 4, "stripped_hunks": 0, "lossy_hunks": 2, "test_target": ["tests/test_request_params/test_file/test_list.py", "tests/test_request_params/test_file/test_optional.py"], "test_target_source": "test_patch", "synthesized": {"graph_observations": true, "repro_observations": true, "test_observations": true, "read_observations": "patch_context", "reasoning_text": true, "repro_script": "generic"}, "note": "Programmatic replay of the reference patch; graph/repro/test observations are synthesized from patch context, not live tool outputs. Read-file observations are patch_context.", "turns": 17, "generator": "gen_programmatic.py v1", "traj_turn": 7, "tool": "run_command", "est_tokens": 1667, "split_from": "fastapi_14953"}}
