ines per call.\n   Use the graph-navigation skill for the exact procedure.\n3. REPRODUCE FIRST. Before editing anything, write a small script\n   under /tmp that triggers the reported failure, and run it with\n   run_command. Confirm you see the reported failure. If you cannot\n   reproduce it, your localization is wrong: go back to step 2. Use\n   the reproduce-first skill for the exact procedure.\n4. FIX. Make one small edit at a time with edit_file (prefer it over\n   write_file, which rewrites whole files). Re-run the /tmp repro\n   after each edit. One hypothesis at a time: if a fix does not change\n   the repro outcome, revert it before trying the next idea.\n5. VERIFY (narrow to wide). Run the narrowest relevant test first\n   (single test, quiet, short traceback), then the containing test\n   file, then the affected package directory. After every pytest run,\n   use the test-summary skill: never paste raw pytest output into\n   your reasoning, condense it to a short failure summary first.\n   Stop expanding the test scope once the repro passes and the\n   directly related tests pass.\n6. CLEAN AND SUBMIT. Delete any scratch files under /workspace (there\n   should be none), run get_status to confirm the diff touches only\n   library code, then call submit_patch. get_status and submit_patch\n   are free and do not count toward the tool-call budget.\n\n## Budget discipline\n\n- You have about 5 minutes and about 50 tool calls per task. Poll\n  get_status every 8-10 calls. If fewer than 10 calls remain, stop\n  exploring and submit the best patch you have.\n- If the same edit fails 3 times, or the same test fails 3 times with\n  no change, stop and submit the best patch instead of looping.\n- Keep the context small: short reads, short repro output, summarized\n  test output. The context ceiling is 32,768 tokens and a flooded\n  context is a failed task."}, {"role": "user", "content": "\u2796 Drop support for `pydantic.v1`\n\n\u2796 Drop support for `pydantic.v1`\r\n\r\nWhen trying to use a `pydantic.v1` model with FastAPI, an error is raised."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in fastapi/routing.py (hunk at line 431).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"fastapi/routing.py\", \"old_text\": \"        )\\n        if solved_result.errors:\\n            raise WebSocketRequestValidationError(\\n                _normalize_errors(solved_result.errors),\\n                endpoint_ctx=endpoint_ctx,\\n            )\\n        assert dependant.call is not None, \\\"dependant.call must be a function\\\"\", \"new_text\": \"        )\\n        if solved_result.errors:\\n            raise WebSocketRequestValidationError(\\n                solved_result.errors,\\n                endpoint_ctx=endpoint_ctx,\\n            )\\n        assert dependant.call is not None, \\\"dependant.call must be a function\\\"\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Edit applied."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in fastapi/routing.py (hunk at line 566).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"fastapi/routing.py\", \"old_text\": \"            )\\n            response_name = \\\"Response_\\\" + self.unique_id\\n            if annotation_is_pydantic_v1(self.response_model):\\n                warnings.warn(\\n                    \\\"pydantic.v1 is deprecated and will soon stop being supported by FastAPI.\\\"\\n                    f\\\" Please update the response model {self.response_model!r}.\\\",\\n                    category=FastAPIDeprecationWarning,\\n                    stacklevel=4,\\n                )\\n            self.response_field = create_model_field(\\n                name=response_name,\", \"new_text\": \"            )\\n            response_name = \\\"Response_\\\" + self.unique_id\\n            if annotation_is_pydantic_v1(self.response_model):\\n                raise PydanticV1NotSupportedError(\\n                    \\\"pydantic.v1 models are no longer supported by FastAPI.\\\"\\n                    f\\\" Please update the response model {self.response_model!r}.\\\"\\n                )\\n            self.response_field = create_model_field(\\n                name=response_name,\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Edit applied."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in fastapi/routing.py (hunk at line 604).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"fastapi/routing.py\", \"old_text\": \"                )\\n                response_name = f\\\"Response_{additional_status_code}_{self.unique_id}\\\"\\n                if annotation_is_pydantic_v1(model):\\n                    warnings.warn(\\n                        \\\"pydantic.v1 is deprecated and will soon stop being supported by FastAPI.\\\"\\n                        f\\\" In responses={{}}, please update {model}.\\\",\\n                        category=FastAPIDeprecationWarning,\\n                        stacklevel=4,\\n                    )\\n                response_field = create_model_field(\\n                    name=response_name, type_=model, mode=\\\"serialization\\\"\", \"new_text\": \"                )\\n                response_name = f\\\"Response_{additional_status_code}_{self.unique_id}\\\"\\n                if annotation_is_pydantic_v1(model):\\n                    raise PydanticV1NotSupportedError(\\n                        \\\"pydantic.v1 models are no longer supported by FastAPI.\\\"\\n                        f\\\" In responses={{}}, please update {model}.\\\"\\n                    )\\n                response_field = create_model_field(\\n                    name=response_name, type_=model, mode=\\\"serialization\\\"\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Edit applied."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in fastapi/utils.py (hunk at line 6).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"fastapi/utils.py\", \"old_text\": \"    Any,\\n    Optional,\\n    Union,\\n    cast,\\n)\\nfrom weakref import WeakKeyDictionary\\n\", \"new_text\": \"    Any,\\n    Optional,\\n    Union,\\n)\\nfrom weakref import WeakKeyDictionary\\n\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}], "metadata": {"source": "programmatic", "instance_id": "fastapi_14609", "repo": "fastapi/fastapi", "base_commit": "1b3bea8b6be2220cc1544653ea785d2134fcb35b", "files": ["docs_src/request_form_models/tutorial002_pv1_an_py39.py", "docs_src/request_form_models/tutorial002_pv1_py39.py", "fastapi/_compat/__init__.py", "fastapi/_compat/main.py", "fastapi/_compat/may_v1.py", "fastapi/_compat/model_field.py", "fastapi/_compat/shared.py", "fastapi/_compat/v1.py", "fastapi/_compat/v2.py", "fastapi/datastructures.py", "fastapi/dependencies/utils.py", "fastapi/encoders.py", "fastapi/exceptions.py", "fastapi/openapi/models.py", "fastapi/openapi/utils.py", "fastapi/param_functions.py", "fastapi/params.py", "fastapi/routing.py", "fastapi/temp_pydantic_v1_params.py", "fastapi/utils.py"], "symbols": ["FormData", "login", "get_cached_model_fields", "_is_undefined"], "n_hunks": 92, "n_files": 20, "stripped_hunks": 0, "lossy_hunks": 0, "test_target": ["tests/test_compat.py", "tests/test_compat_params_v1.py"], "test_target_source": "test_patch", "synthesized": {"graph_observations": true, "repro_observations": true, "test_observations": true, "read_observations": "snapshot", "reasoning_text": true, "repro_script": "generic"}, "note": "Programmatic replay of the reference patch; graph/repro/test observations are synthesized from patch context, not live tool outputs. Read-file observations are snapshot.", "turns": 104, "generator": "gen_programmatic.py v1", "traj_turn": 96, "tool": "edit_file", "est_tokens": 1902, "split_from": "fastapi_14609"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "# Coder \u2014 system prompt\n\nYou are an offline autonomous Python coding agent working in /workspace, a\nrepository snapshot at the commit just before the fix. You resolve one\nissue per session: diagnose the bug or feature request, write the\nsmallest patch that makes the hidden tests pass, and submit it.\n\nThe hidden tests are applied AFTER your patch in a clean container. Test\nfiles and runner config are reset there, so editing them here is wasted\nwork and stripped from your patch. Fix library code only.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- NEVER edit, create, or delete test files or runner config: tests,\n  conftest, pytest.ini, pyproject.toml, tox.ini, setup.cfg, and any\n  packaging or test-runner file. If a fix seems to need a test change,\n  you are wrong: re-read the issue and the library code.\n- NEVER write scratch scripts, repro scripts, or temp files under\n  /workspace: anything left there enters the patch diff. Scratch work\n  goes under /tmp only, and is deleted before submit.\n- Minimal patches only: change the fewest lines that fix the issue,\n  preserve existing style and names, do not refactor, do not add\n  features the issue does not ask for.\n- Never guess an API or symbol: read the code first.\n- Do not browse the network: there is none. All tools are offline.\n\n## Workflow (follow in order, do not skip steps)\n\n1. READ THE TASK. Extract: what fails, the exact error or wrong\n   behaviour, and any failing test names mentioned in the issue.\n2. LOCALIZE (graph first). Call the code_analyzer sub-agent once with\n   the issue text and the failing test names. Then use the graph tools\n   yourself on the SYMBOLS it returns (never natural-language queries):\n   search_similar_code for symbol lookup, get_code_neighbors for\n   callers and callees, get_code_subgraph for the surrounding call\n   graph. Only then read_file the 1-3 files that matter. Skim, do not\n   dump whole files: read_file returns at most 150 lines per call.\n   Use the graph-navigation skill for the exact procedure.\n3. REPRODUCE FIRST. Before editing anything, write a small script\n   under /tmp that triggers the reported failure, and run it with\n   run_command. Confirm you see the reported failure. If you cannot\n   reproduce it, your localization is wrong: go back to step 2. Use\n   the reproduce-first skill for the exact procedure.\n4. FIX. Make one small edit at a time with edit_file (prefer it over\n   write_file, which rewrites whole files). Re-run the /tmp repro\n   after each edit. One hypothesis at a time: if a fix does not change\n   the repro outcome, revert it before trying the next idea.\n5. VERIFY (narrow to wide). Run the narrowest relevant test first\n   (single test, quiet, short traceback), then the containing test\n   file, then the affected package directory. After every pytest run,\n   use the test-summary skill: never paste raw pytest output into\n   your reasoning, condense it to a short failure summary first.\n   Stop expanding the test scope once the repro passes and the\n   directly related tests pass.\n6. CLEAN AND SUBMIT. Delete any scratch files under /workspace (there\n   should be none), run get_status to confirm the diff touches only\n   library code, then call submit_patch. get_status and submit_patch\n   are free and do not count toward the tool-call budget.\n\n## Budget discipline\n\n- You have about 5 minutes and about 50 tool calls per task. Poll\n  get_status every 8-10 calls. If fewer than 10 calls remain, stop\n  exploring and submit the best patch you have.\n- If the same edit fails 3 times, or the same test fails 3 times with\n  no change, stop and submit the best patch instead of looping.\n- Keep the context small: short reads, short repro output, summarized\n  test output. The context ceiling is 32,768 tokens and a flooded\n  context is a failed task."}, {"role": "user", "content": "perf: reduce Console and RichHandler import time by deferring unused imports\n\n## E2E Results\n\n| Import | master | This PR | Speedup |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `from rich.console import Console` | 78.1ms | 52.2ms | **1.50x faster** |\n| `from rich.logging import RichHandler` | 99.4ms | 56.9ms | **1.75x faster** |\n| `import rich` | 18.2ms | 18.3ms | (already lean) |\n\n## Summary\n\nDefer module-level imports that are only needed in specific code paths, move annotation-only imports to `TYPE_CHECKING`, and remove dead code:\n\n| File | Change | Savings |\n|---|---|---|\n| `logging.py` | Defer `Traceback` to `emit()` (only when `rich_tracebacks=True`) | ~20ms |\n| `logging.py` | `from __future__ import annotations` + `TYPE_CHECKING` for `Console`, `ConsoleRenderable`, `Highlighter`, `FormatTimeCallable` | ~6ms |\n| `logging.py` | Replace `pathlib.Path` \u2192 `os.path.basename` (also a minor runtime win) | ~4-5ms |\n| `console.py` | Eliminate `import inspect`; replace `isclass` \u2192 `isinstance(x, type)`, `currentframe` \u2192 `sys._getframe` | ~10ms |\n| `console.py` | Defer `pretty` to `Console.print()` | ~3-5ms |\n| `console.py` | Defer `scope` to `Console.log()` (only when `log_locals=True`) | ~3-5ms |\n| `console.py` | Defer `getpass` to `Console.input()` (only when `password=True`) | ~2ms |\n| `console.py` | Defer `html.escape` and `zlib` to export methods | ~2.3ms |\n| `console.py` | Remove dead `_svg_hash` function (unused since 113997ac, fixes latent NameError) | cleanup |\n| `segment.py` | Remove dead `logging` import (`getLogger` assigned but never used) | ~2-3ms |\n| `theme.py` | Defer `configparser` to `Theme.from_file()` | ~1.5ms |\n| `syntax.py` | Move `Console`, `ConsoleOptions`, `JustifyMethod`, `RenderResult` to `TYPE_CHECKING` | eliminates console.py from syntax import chain |\n| `protocol.py` | Replace `from inspect import isclass` \u2192 `isinstance(x, type)` | prepares for dataclasses removal |\n| `repr.py` | Defer `import inspect` to `auto_rich_repr()` | prepares for dataclasses removal |\n\nAll deferred imports are still available at runtime \u2014 they're loaded when the code path that needs them is actually hit. The `TYPE_CHECKING` imports are annotation-only and never needed at runtime (files use `from __future__ import annotations`).\n\n## Benchmarks\n\nEnvironment: Standard_D2s_v5 (non-burstable), CPython 3.13.13, hyperfine --min-runs 30\n\n### Per-module breakdown (this PR)\n\n| Module | Time |\n|---|---|\n| `import rich` | 18.2ms |\n| `Console` | 52.5ms |\n| `RichHandler` | 56.4ms |\n| `Syntax` | 64.7ms |\n| `Traceback` | 92.5ms |\n| `Markdown` | 103.0ms |\n\n## Motivation\n\npip vendors Rich, and `Console`/`RichHandler` are imported on every `pip` invocation. These savings directly improve startup time for pip and every other tool that imports Rich.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `pytest tests/` \u2014 952 passed, 25 skipped\n- `mypy -p rich --no-incremental` \u2014 no issues\n- `black --check` \u2014 passes\n\n## Disclosure\n\nThis PR was developed with AI assistance (codeflash-agent). Benchmarked in a controlled environment (dedicated non-burstable Azure VM). All tests verified across multiple Python implementations."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Before editing anything, I'll write a small repro script under /tmp that triggers the reported failure.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"write_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"/tmp/repro_rich_4070.py\", \"content\": \"\\\"\\\"\\\"Repro for rich_4070 (programmatic replay; generic script).\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nimport sys\\nsys.path.insert(0, \\\"/workspace\\\")\\n\\nfrom rich._emoji_replace import _emoji_replace  # noqa: F401\\n\\n# Reported failure (from the issue):\\n# perf: reduce Console and RichHandler import time by deferring unused imports ## E2E Results | Import | master | This PR | Speedup | |---|---|---|---| | `from rich.console import Console` | 78.1ms | 52...\\n# A faithful repro needs the exact failing call from the issue, so\\n# this script confirms the module imports and the symbol exists;\\n# the failure itself is triggered from the issue's example.\\nprint(\\\"repro ready:\\\", _emoji_replace)\\n\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "File written."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Running the repro to confirm the reported failure.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"run_command\", \"arguments\": {\"command\": \"python /tmp/repro_rich_4070.py\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}], "metadata": {"source": "programmatic", "instance_id": "rich_4070", "repo": "Textualize/rich", "base_commit": "fc41075a3206d2a5fd846c6f41c4d2becab814fa", "files": ["rich/_emoji_replace.py", "rich/console.py", "rich/emoji.py", "rich/logging.py", "rich/protocol.py", "rich/repr.py", "rich/segment.py", "rich/syntax.py", "rich/theme.py"], "symbols": ["_emoji_replace", "render", "_collect_renderables", "is_expandable"], "n_hunks": 33, "n_files": 9, "stripped_hunks": 0, "lossy_hunks": 0, "test_target": ["tests/test_console.py", "tests/test_syntax.py"], "test_target_source": "test_patch", "synthesized": {"graph_observations": true, "repro_observations": true, "test_observations": true, "read_observations": "snapshot", "reasoning_text": true, "repro_script": "generic"}, "note": "Programmatic replay of the reference patch; graph/repro/test observations are synthesized from patch context, not live tool outputs. Read-file observations are snapshot.", "turns": 45, "generator": "gen_programmatic.py v1", "traj_turn": 7, "tool": "run_command", "est_tokens": 1983, "split_from": "rich_4070"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "# Coder \u2014 system prompt\n\nYou are an offline autonomous Python coding agent working in /workspace, a\nrepository snapshot at the commit just before the fix. You resolve one\nissue per session: diagnose the bug or feature request, write the\nsmallest patch that makes the hidden tests pass, and submit it.\n\nThe hidden tests are applied AFTER your patch in a clean container. Test\nfiles and runner config are reset there, so editing them here is wasted\nwork and stripped from your patch. Fix library code only.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- NEVER edit, create, or delete test files or runner config: tests,\n  conftest, pytest.ini, pyproject.toml, tox.ini, setup.cfg, and any\n  packaging or test-runner file. If a fix seems to need a test change,\n  you are wrong: re-read the issue and the library code.\n- NEVER write scratch scripts, repro scripts, or temp files under\n  /workspace: anything left there enters the patch diff. Scratch work\n  goes under /tmp only, and is deleted before submit.\n- Minimal patches only: change the fewest lines that fix the issue,\n  preserve existing style and names, do not refactor, do not add\n  features the issue does not ask for.\n- Never guess an API or symbol: read the code first.\n- Do not browse the network: there is none. All tools are offline.\n\n## Workflow (follow in order, do not skip steps)\n\n1. READ THE TASK. Extract: what fails, the exact error or wrong\n   behaviour, and any failing test names mentioned in the issue.\n2. LOCALIZE (graph first). Call the code_analyzer sub-agent once with\n   the issue text and the failing test names. Then use the graph tools\n   yourself on the SYMBOLS it returns (never natural-language queries):\n   search_similar_code for symbol lookup, get_code_neighbors for\n   callers and callees, get_code_subgraph for the surrounding call\n   graph. Only then read_file the 1-3 files that matter. Skim, do not\n   dump whole files: read_file returns at most 150 lines per call.\n   Use the graph-navigation skill for the exact procedure.\n3. REPRODUCE FIRST. Before editing anything, write a small script\n   under /tmp that triggers the reported failure, and run it with\n   run_command. Confirm you see the reported failure. If you cannot\n   reproduce it, your localization is wrong: go back to step 2. Use\n   the reproduce-first skill for the exact procedure.\n4. FIX. Make one small edit at a time with edit_file (prefer it over\n   write_file, which rewrites whole files). Re-run the /tmp repro\n   after each edit. One hypothesis at a time: if a fix does not change\n   the repro outcome, revert it before trying the next idea.\n5. VERIFY (narrow to wide). Run the narrowest relevant test first\n   (single test, quiet, short traceback), then the containing test\n   file, then the affected package directory. After every pytest run,\n   use the test-summary skill: never paste raw pytest output into\n   your reasoning, condense it to a short failure summary first.\n   Stop expanding the test scope once the repro passes and the\n   directly related tests pass.\n6. CLEAN AND SUBMIT. Delete any scratch files under /workspace (there\n   should be none), run get_status to confirm the diff touches only\n   library code, then call submit_patch. get_status and submit_patch\n   are free and do not count toward the tool-call budget.\n\n## Budget discipline\n\n- You have about 5 minutes and about 50 tool calls per task. Poll\n  get_status every 8-10 calls. If fewer than 10 calls remain, stop\n  exploring and submit the best patch you have.\n- If the same edit fails 3 times, or the same test fails 3 times with\n  no change, stop and submit the best patch instead of looping.\n- Keep the context small: short reads, short repro output, summarized\n  test output. The context ceiling is 32,768 tokens and a flooded\n  context is a failed task."}, {"role": "user", "content": "\ud83d\udc1b Fix parsing extra `Form` parameter list\n\n## Description\r\n\r\nCurrently if we declare Form parameters as model with `extra=\"allow\"` and then pass extra parameter with multiple values, only last value of such parameter will be passed to model validator:\r\n\r\n```py\r\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, Form\r\nfrom fastapi.testclient import TestClient\r\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\r\n\r\napp = FastAPI()\r\n\r\n\r\nclass Model(BaseModel):\r\n    param: str\r\n\r\n    model_config = {\"extra\": \"allow\"}\r\n\r\n@app.post(\"/\")\r\nasync def model_with_extra(data: Model = Form()):\r\n    return data\r\n\r\n\r\ndef test_pass_extra_list():\r\n    client = TestClient(app)\r\n    resp = client.post(\r\n        \"/\",\r\n        data={\r\n            \"param\": \"123\",\r\n            \"param2\": [\"456\", \"789\"],  # Pass a list of values as extra parameter\r\n        },\r\n    )\r\n    assert resp.status_code == 200\r\n    assert resp.json() == {\r\n        \"param\": \"123\",\r\n        \"param2\": [\"456\", \"789\"],\r\n    }\r\n    # AssertionError: assert\r\n    # {'param': '123', 'param2': '789'}\r\n    # ==\r\n    # {'param': '123', 'param2': ['456', '789']}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe part of code that adds extra parameters is:\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/fastapi/fastapi/blob/972a967d5d3fa24d450637c36c6b7ad1f1d8c1b6/fastapi/dependencies/utils.py#L901-L903\r\n\r\nThe problem is in `received_body.items()` - it returns only last value for each key.\r\nTo fix this we need to iterate over all keys in `received_body` and get the values using `received_body.getlist(key)`:\r\n\r\n```py\r\n    for key in received_body.keys():\r\n        if key not in values:\r\n            param_values = received_body.getlist(key)\r\n            if len(param_values) == 1:\r\n                values[key] = param_values[0]\r\n            else:\r\n                values[key] = param_values\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Alternative implementation\r\n\r\nThis solution parses single value as string\r\n\r\n`extra=string` -> `{\"extra\": \"string\"}`\r\n\r\nbut multiple values as list of strings\r\n\r\n`extra=string1&extra=string2` -> `{\"extra\": [\"string1\", \"string2\"]}`\r\n\r\nWe can consider always parse extra parameters as lists\r\n\r\n```py\r\n    for key in received_body.keys():\r\n        if key not in values:\r\n            values[key] = received_body.getlist(key)\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis way for single value we would also have list with one element:\r\n\r\n`extra=string` -> `{\"extra\": [\"string\"]}`\r\n\r\nIt's more consistent, but probably not what people expect for single value.\r\n\r\nI think we should go with current implementation (single value -> single string, multiple values -> list of strings)\r\n\r\n## Other parameters\r\n\r\nQuery\\Header\\Cookie parameters likely have the same issue, I will look at it a bit later and open separate PR to fix this"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "I'll localize with the graph tools first, starting from the symbols named in the issue.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"search_similar_code\", \"arguments\": {\"symbol\": \"process_fn\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "{\n \"symbol\": \"process_fn\",\n \"matches\": [\n  {\n   \"file\": \"fastapi/dependencies/utils.py\",\n   \"line\": 893,\n   \"kind\": \"definition\"\n  }\n ]\n}"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "process_fn is defined in fastapi/dependencies/utils.py. Checking its callers and callees before reading any file.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"get_code_neighbors\", \"arguments\": {\"symbol\": \"process_fn\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "{\n \"symbol\": \"process_fn\",\n \"callers\": [\n  \"items\",\n  \"keys\",\n  \"getlist\"\n ],\n \"callees\": [\n  \"items\",\n  \"keys\",\n  \"getlist\"\n ],\n \"note\": \"neighbors derived from symbols co-occurring in the patch\"\n}"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Getting the surrounding call graph so I read only the files that matter.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"get_code_subgraph\", \"arguments\": {\"symbol\": \"process_fn\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}], "metadata": {"source": "programmatic", "instance_id": "fastapi_14303", "repo": "fastapi/fastapi", "base_commit": "740ec2787b25e59f7aa9e3df1db09d4489c22718", "files": ["fastapi/dependencies/utils.py"], "symbols": ["process_fn", "items", "keys", "getlist"], "n_hunks": 1, "n_files": 1, "stripped_hunks": 0, "lossy_hunks": 0, "test_target": ["tests/test_forms_single_model.py"], "test_target_source": "test_patch", "synthesized": {"graph_observations": true, "repro_observations": true, "test_observations": true, "read_observations": "snapshot", "reasoning_text": true, "repro_script": "generic"}, "note": "Programmatic replay of the reference patch; graph/repro/test observations are synthesized from patch context, not live tool outputs. Read-file observations are snapshot.", "turns": 11, "generator": "gen_programmatic.py v1", "traj_turn": 2, "tool": "get_code_subgraph", "est_tokens": 1830, "split_from": "fastapi_14303"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "# Coder \u2014 system prompt\n\nYou are an offline autonomous Python coding agent working in /workspace, a\nrepository snapshot at the commit just before the fix. You resolve one\nissue per session: diagnose the bug or feature request, write the\nsmallest patch that makes the hidden tests pass, and submit it.\n\nThe hidden tests are applied AFTER your patch in a clean container. Test\nfiles and runner config are reset there, so editing them here is wasted\nwork and stripped from your patch. Fix library code only.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- NEVER edit, create, or delete test files or runner config: tests,\n  conftest, pytest.ini, pyproject.toml, tox.ini, setup.cfg, and any\n  packaging or test-runner file. If a fix seems to need a test change,\n  you are wrong: re-read the issue and the library code.\n- NEVER write scratch scripts, repro scripts, or temp files under\n  /workspace: anything left there enters the patch diff. Scratch work\n  goes under /tmp only, and is deleted before submit.\n- Minimal patches only: change the fewest lines that fix the issue,\n  preserve existing style and names, do not refactor, do not add\n  features the issue does not ask for.\n- Never guess an API or symbol: read the code first.\n- Do not browse the network: there is none. All tools are offline.\n\n## Workflow (follow in order, do not skip steps)\n\n1. READ THE TASK. Extract: what fails, the exact error or wrong\n   behaviour, and any failing test names mentioned in the issue.\n2. LOCALIZE (graph first). Call the code_analyzer sub-agent once with\n   the issue text and the failing test names. Then use the graph tools\n   yourself on the SYMBOLS it returns (never natural-language queries):\n   search_similar_code for symbol lookup, get_code_neighbors for\n   callers and callees, get_code_subgraph for the surrounding call\n   graph. Only then read_file the 1-3 files that matter. Skim, do not\n   dump whole files: read_file returns at most 150 lines per call.\n   Use the graph-navigation skill for the exact procedure.\n3. REPRODUCE FIRST. Before editing anything, write a small script\n   under /tmp that triggers the reported failure, and run it with\n   run_command. Confirm you see the reported failure. If you cannot\n   reproduce it, your localization is wrong: go back to step 2. Use\n   the reproduce-first skill for the exact procedure.\n4. FIX. Make one small edit at a time with edit_file (prefer it over\n   write_file, which rewrites whole files). Re-run the /tmp repro\n   after each edit. One hypothesis at a time: if a fix does not change\n   the repro outcome, revert it before trying the next idea.\n5. VERIFY (narrow to wide). Run the narrowest relevant test first\n   (single test, quiet, short traceback), then the containing test\n   file, then the affected package directory. After every pytest run,\n   use the test-summary skill: never paste raw pytest output into\n   your reasoning, condense it to a short failure summary first.\n   Stop expanding the test scope once the repro passes and the\n   directly related tests pass.\n6. CLEAN AND SUBMIT. Delete any scratch files under /workspace (there\n   should be none), run get_status to confirm the diff touches only\n   library code, then call submit_patch. get_status and submit_patch\n   are free and do not count toward the tool-call budget.\n\n## Budget discipline\n\n- You have about 5 minutes and about 50 tool calls per task. Poll\n  get_status every 8-10 calls. If fewer than 10 calls remain, stop\n  exploring and submit the best patch you have.\n- If the same edit fails 3 times, or the same test fails 3 times with\n  no change, stop and submit the best patch instead of looping.\n- Keep the context small: short reads, short repro output, summarized\n  test output. The context ceiling is 32,768 tokens and a flooded\n  context is a failed task."}, {"role": "user", "content": "\ud83d\udc1b Fix internal Pydantic v1 compatibility (warnings) for Python 3.14 and Pydantic 2.12.1\n\nThe Python 3.14 tests started failing with the Pydantic v.2.12.1 release, because of the combination of https://github.com/fastapi/fastapi/pull/14168 and https://github.com/pydantic/pydantic/pull/12367.\r\n\r\nBasically we were importing Pydantic `v1` functionality even when the user had (only) Pydantic v2 installed. This was done in https://github.com/fastapi/fastapi/pull/14168 to support more easy migration from v1 to v2 by allowing mixing versions within the same app.\r\n\r\nHere, we prevent importing any `v1` functionality by creating dummy classes for Python 3.14 / Pydantic 2.12.0 onwards, which [drops support](https://github.com/pydantic/pydantic/releases/tag/v2.12.0) for `v1` and emits a [warning](https://github.com/pydantic/pydantic/pull/12367) when importing `v1` functionality.\r\n\r\n### TODO\r\n\r\n- [x] fix coverage\r\n- [ ] check warning is still shown to users at some point, and not just swallowed by this code."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in fastapi/temp_pydantic_v1_params.py (hunk at line 5).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"fastapi/temp_pydantic_v1_params.py\", \"old_text\": \"from fastapi.params import ParamTypes\\nfrom typing_extensions import Annotated, deprecated\\n\\nfrom ._compat.shared import PYDANTIC_VERSION_MINOR_TUPLE\\nfrom ._compat.v1 import FieldInfo, Undefined\\n\\n_Unset: Any = Undefined\\n\", \"new_text\": \"from fastapi.params import ParamTypes\\nfrom typing_extensions import Annotated, deprecated\\n\\nfrom ._compat.may_v1 import FieldInfo, Undefined\\nfrom ._compat.shared import PYDANTIC_VERSION_MINOR_TUPLE\\n\\n_Unset: Any = Undefined\\n\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Edit applied."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in fastapi/utils.py (hunk at line 25).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"fastapi/utils.py\", \"old_text\": \"    Validator,\\n    annotation_is_pydantic_v1,\\n    lenient_issubclass,\\n    v1,\\n)\\nfrom fastapi.datastructures import DefaultPlaceholder, DefaultType\\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\", \"new_text\": \"    Validator,\\n    annotation_is_pydantic_v1,\\n    lenient_issubclass,\\n    may_v1,\\n)\\nfrom fastapi.datastructures import DefaultPlaceholder, DefaultType\\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Edit applied."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in fastapi/utils.py (hunk at line 87).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"fastapi/utils.py\", \"old_text\": \") -> ModelField:\\n    class_validators = class_validators or {}\\n\\n    v1_model_config = v1.BaseConfig\\n    v1_field_info = field_info or v1.FieldInfo()\\n    v1_kwargs = {\\n        \\\"name\\\": name,\\n        \\\"field_info\\\": v1_field_info,\", \"new_text\": \") -> ModelField:\\n    class_validators = class_validators or {}\\n\\n    v1_model_config = may_v1.BaseConfig\\n    v1_field_info = field_info or may_v1.FieldInfo()\\n    v1_kwargs = {\\n        \\\"name\\\": name,\\n        \\\"field_info\\\": v1_field_info,\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Edit applied."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in fastapi/utils.py (hunk at line 102).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"fastapi/utils.py\", \"old_text\": \"\\n    if (\\n        annotation_is_pydantic_v1(type_)\\n        or isinstance(field_info, v1.FieldInfo)\\n        or version == \\\"1\\\"\\n    ):\\n        try:\\n            return v1.ModelField(**v1_kwargs)  # type: ignore[no-any-return]\\n        except RuntimeError:\", \"new_text\": \"\\n    if (\\n        annotation_is_pydantic_v1(type_)\\n        or isinstance(field_info, may_v1.FieldInfo)\\n        or version == \\\"1\\\"\\n    ):\\n        from fastapi._compat import v1\\n\\n        try:\\n            return v1.ModelField(**v1_kwargs)  # type: ignore[no-any-return]\\n        except RuntimeError:\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}], "metadata": {"source": "programmatic", "instance_id": "fastapi_14186", "repo": "fastapi/fastapi", "base_commit": "6e49dc029541aaa896fa63c47647ccd3f066df67", "files": ["fastapi/_compat/__init__.py", "fastapi/_compat/main.py", "fastapi/_compat/may_v1.py", "fastapi/_compat/shared.py", "fastapi/_compat/v1.py", "fastapi/_compat/v2.py", "fastapi/dependencies/utils.py", "fastapi/encoders.py", "fastapi/temp_pydantic_v1_params.py", "fastapi/utils.py"], "symbols": ["get_cached_model_fields", "_is_undefined", "_get_model_config", "_model_dump"], "n_hunks": 51, "n_files": 10, "stripped_hunks": 0, "lossy_hunks": 1, "test_target": ["tests/test_compat.py", "tests/test_get_model_definitions_formfeed_escape.py"], "test_target_source": "test_patch", "synthesized": {"graph_observations": true, "repro_observations": true, "test_observations": true, "read_observations": "patch_context", "reasoning_text": true, "repro_script": "generic"}, "note": "Programmatic replay of the reference patch; graph/repro/test observations are synthesized from patch context, not live tool outputs. Read-file observations are patch_context.", "turns": 63, "generator": "gen_programmatic.py v1", "traj_turn": 56, "tool": "edit_file", "est_tokens": 1863, "split_from": "fastapi_14186"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "# Coder \u2014 system prompt\n\nYou are an offline autonomous Python coding agent working in /workspace, a\nrepository snapshot at the commit just before the fix. You resolve one\nissue per session: diagnose the bug or feature request, write the\nsmallest patch that makes the hidden tests pass, and submit it.\n\nThe hidden tests are applied AFTER your patch in a clean container. Test\nfiles and runner config are reset there, so editing them here is wasted\nwork and stripped from your patch. Fix library code only.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- NEVER edit, create, or delete test files or runner config: tests,\n  conftest, pytest.ini, pyproject.toml, tox.ini, setup.cfg, and any\n  packaging or test-runner file. If a fix seems to need a test change,\n  you are wrong: re-read the issue and the library code.\n- NEVER write scratch scripts, repro scripts, or temp files under\n  /workspace: anything left there enters the patch diff. Scratch work\n  goes under /tmp only, and is deleted before submit.\n- Minimal patches only: change the fewest lines that fix the issue,\n  preserve existing style and names, do not refactor, do not add\n  features the issue does not ask for.\n- Never guess an API or symbol: read the code first.\n- Do not browse the network: there is none. All tools are offline.\n\n## Workflow (follow in order, do not skip steps)\n\n1. READ THE TASK. Extract: what fails, the exact error or wrong\n   behaviour, and any failing test names mentioned in the issue.\n2. LOCALIZE (graph first). Call the code_analyzer sub-agent once with\n   the issue text and the failing test names. Then use the graph tools\n   yourself on the SYMBOLS it returns (never natural-language queries):\n   search_similar_code for symbol lookup, get_code_neighbors for\n   callers and callees, get_code_subgraph for the surrounding call\n   graph. Only then read_file the 1-3 files that matter. Skim, do not\n   dump whole files: read_file returns at most 150 lines per call.\n   Use the graph-navigation skill for the exact procedure.\n3. REPRODUCE FIRST. Before editing anything, write a small script\n   under /tmp that triggers the reported failure, and run it with\n   run_command. Confirm you see the reported failure. If you cannot\n   reproduce it, your localization is wrong: go back to step 2. Use\n   the reproduce-first skill for the exact procedure.\n4. FIX. Make one small edit at a time with edit_file (prefer it over\n   write_file, which rewrites whole files). Re-run the /tmp repro\n   after each edit. One hypothesis at a time: if a fix does not change\n   the repro outcome, revert it before trying the next idea.\n5. VERIFY (narrow to wide). Run the narrowest relevant test first\n   (single test, quiet, short traceback), then the containing test\n   file, then the affected package directory. After every pytest run,\n   use the test-summary skill: never paste raw pytest output into\n   your reasoning, condense it to a short failure summary first.\n   Stop expanding the test scope once the repro passes and the\n   directly related tests pass.\n6. CLEAN AND SUBMIT. Delete any scratch files under /workspace (there\n   should be none), run get_status to confirm the diff touches only\n   library code, then call submit_patch. get_status and submit_patch\n   are free and do not count toward the tool-call budget.\n\n## Budget discipline\n\n- You have about 5 minutes and about 50 tool calls per task. Poll\n  get_status every 8-10 calls. If fewer than 10 calls remain, stop\n  exploring and submit the best patch you have.\n- If the same edit fails 3 times, or the same test fails 3 times with\n  no change, stop and submit the best patch instead of looping.\n- Keep the context small: short reads, short repro output, summarized\n  test output. The context ceiling is 32,768 tokens and a flooded\n  context is a failed task."}, {"role": "user", "content": "report fine grained error locations\n\nAdds PEP 657 finely grained error loca