ines per call.\n   Use the graph-navigation skill for the exact procedure.\n3. REPRODUCE FIRST. Before editing anything, write a small script\n   under /tmp that triggers the reported failure, and run it with\n   run_command. Confirm you see the reported failure. If you cannot\n   reproduce it, your localization is wrong: go back to step 2. Use\n   the reproduce-first skill for the exact procedure.\n4. FIX. Make one small edit at a time with edit_file (prefer it over\n   write_file, which rewrites whole files). Re-run the /tmp repro\n   after each edit. One hypothesis at a time: if a fix does not change\n   the repro outcome, revert it before trying the next idea.\n5. VERIFY (narrow to wide). Run the narrowest relevant test first\n   (single test, quiet, short traceback), then the containing test\n   file, then the affected package directory. After every pytest run,\n   use the test-summary skill: never paste raw pytest output into\n   your reasoning, condense it to a short failure summary first.\n   Stop expanding the test scope once the repro passes and the\n   directly related tests pass.\n6. CLEAN AND SUBMIT. Delete any scratch files under /workspace (there\n   should be none), run get_status to confirm the diff touches only\n   library code, then call submit_patch. get_status and submit_patch\n   are free and do not count toward the tool-call budget.\n\n## Budget discipline\n\n- You have about 5 minutes and about 50 tool calls per task. Poll\n  get_status every 8-10 calls. If fewer than 10 calls remain, stop\n  exploring and submit the best patch you have.\n- If the same edit fails 3 times, or the same test fails 3 times with\n  no change, stop and submit the best patch instead of looping.\n- Keep the context small: short reads, short repro output, summarized\n  test output. The context ceiling is 32,768 tokens and a flooded\n  context is a failed task."}, {"role": "user", "content": "fix(table): highlight columns added by add_row\n\n## Type of changes\r\n\r\n- [x] Bug fix\r\n- [ ] New feature\r\n- [ ] Documentation / docstrings\r\n- [ ] Tests\r\n- [ ] Other\r\n\r\n## Checklist\r\n\r\n- [x] I've run the latest [black](https://github.com/psf/black) with default args on new code.\r\n- [x] I've updated CHANGELOG.md and CONTRIBUTORS.md where appropriate.\r\n- [x] I've added tests for new code.\r\n- [x] I accept that @willmcgugan may be pedantic in the code review.\r\n\r\n## Description\r\n\r\nFixes #3517. The default value for the `Column.highlight` is False, so where necessary columns are added by the `add_row` method this should default to the `Table.highlight` value."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "I'll localize with the graph tools first, starting from the symbols named in the issue.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"search_similar_code\", \"arguments\": {\"symbol\": \"add_cell\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "{\n \"symbol\": \"add_cell\",\n \"matches\": [\n  {\n   \"file\": \"rich/table.py\",\n   \"line\": 441,\n   \"kind\": \"definition\"\n  }\n ]\n}"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "add_cell is defined in rich/table.py. Checking its callers and callees before reading any file.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"get_code_neighbors\", \"arguments\": {\"symbol\": \"add_cell\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}], "metadata": {"source": "programmatic", "instance_id": "rich_3518", "repo": "Textualize/rich", "base_commit": "5ba9cb56e68dcdf2db50c214d8f1412c8a50761b", "files": ["rich/table.py"], "symbols": ["add_cell", "Column"], "n_hunks": 1, "n_files": 1, "stripped_hunks": 0, "lossy_hunks": 0, "test_target": ["tests/test_table.py"], "test_target_source": "test_patch", "synthesized": {"graph_observations": true, "repro_observations": true, "test_observations": true, "read_observations": "snapshot", "reasoning_text": true, "repro_script": "generic"}, "note": "Programmatic replay of the reference patch; graph/repro/test observations are synthesized from patch context, not live tool outputs. Read-file observations are snapshot.", "turns": 11, "generator": "gen_programmatic.py v1", "traj_turn": 1, "tool": "get_code_neighbors", "est_tokens": 1232, "split_from": "rich_3518"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "# Coder \u2014 system prompt\n\nYou are an offline autonomous Python coding agent working in /workspace, a\nrepository snapshot at the commit just before the fix. You resolve one\nissue per session: diagnose the bug or feature request, write the\nsmallest patch that makes the hidden tests pass, and submit it.\n\nThe hidden tests are applied AFTER your patch in a clean container. Test\nfiles and runner config are reset there, so editing them here is wasted\nwork and stripped from your patch. Fix library code only.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- NEVER edit, create, or delete test files or runner config: tests,\n  conftest, pytest.ini, pyproject.toml, tox.ini, setup.cfg, and any\n  packaging or test-runner file. If a fix seems to need a test change,\n  you are wrong: re-read the issue and the library code.\n- NEVER write scratch scripts, repro scripts, or temp files under\n  /workspace: anything left there enters the patch diff. Scratch work\n  goes under /tmp only, and is deleted before submit.\n- Minimal patches only: change the fewest lines that fix the issue,\n  preserve existing style and names, do not refactor, do not add\n  features the issue does not ask for.\n- Never guess an API or symbol: read the code first.\n- Do not browse the network: there is none. All tools are offline.\n\n## Workflow (follow in order, do not skip steps)\n\n1. READ THE TASK. Extract: what fails, the exact error or wrong\n   behaviour, and any failing test names mentioned in the issue.\n2. LOCALIZE (graph first). Call the code_analyzer sub-agent once with\n   the issue text and the failing test names. Then use the graph tools\n   yourself on the SYMBOLS it returns (never natural-language queries):\n   search_similar_code for symbol lookup, get_code_neighbors for\n   callers and callees, get_code_subgraph for the surrounding call\n   graph. Only then read_file the 1-3 files that matter. Skim, do not\n   dump whole files: read_file returns at most 150 lines per call.\n   Use the graph-navigation skill for the exact procedure.\n3. REPRODUCE FIRST. Before editing anything, write a small script\n   under /tmp that triggers the reported failure, and run it with\n   run_command. Confirm you see the reported failure. If you cannot\n   reproduce it, your localization is wrong: go back to step 2. Use\n   the reproduce-first skill for the exact procedure.\n4. FIX. Make one small edit at a time with edit_file (prefer it over\n   write_file, which rewrites whole files). Re-run the /tmp repro\n   after each edit. One hypothesis at a time: if a fix does not change\n   the repro outcome, revert it before trying the next idea.\n5. VERIFY (narrow to wide). Run the narrowest relevant test first\n   (single test, quiet, short traceback), then the containing test\n   file, then the affected package directory. After every pytest run,\n   use the test-summary skill: never paste raw pytest output into\n   your reasoning, condense it to a short failure summary first.\n   Stop expanding the test scope once the repro passes and the\n   directly related tests pass.\n6. CLEAN AND SUBMIT. Delete any scratch files under /workspace (there\n   should be none), run get_status to confirm the diff touches only\n   library code, then call submit_patch. get_status and submit_patch\n   are free and do not count toward the tool-call budget.\n\n## Budget discipline\n\n- You have about 5 minutes and about 50 tool calls per task. Poll\n  get_status every 8-10 calls. If fewer than 10 calls remain, stop\n  exploring and submit the best patch you have.\n- If the same edit fails 3 times, or the same test fails 3 times with\n  no change, stop and submit the best patch instead of looping.\n- Keep the context small: short reads, short repro output, summarized\n  test output. The context ceiling is 32,768 tokens and a flooded\n  context is a failed task."}, {"role": "user", "content": "perf: reduce Console and RichHandler import time by deferring unused imports\n\n## E2E Results\n\n| Import | master | This PR | Speedup |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `from rich.console import Console` | 78.1ms | 52.2ms | **1.50x faster** |\n| `from rich.logging import RichHandler` | 99.4ms | 56.9ms | **1.75x faster** |\n| `import rich` | 18.2ms | 18.3ms | (already lean) |\n\n## Summary\n\nDefer module-level imports that are only needed in specific code paths, move annotation-only imports to `TYPE_CHECKING`, and remove dead code:\n\n| File | Change | Savings |\n|---|---|---|\n| `logging.py` | Defer `Traceback` to `emit()` (only when `rich_tracebacks=True`) | ~20ms |\n| `logging.py` | `from __future__ import annotations` + `TYPE_CHECKING` for `Console`, `ConsoleRenderable`, `Highlighter`, `FormatTimeCallable` | ~6ms |\n| `logging.py` | Replace `pathlib.Path` \u2192 `os.path.basename` (also a minor runtime win) | ~4-5ms |\n| `console.py` | Eliminate `import inspect`; replace `isclass` \u2192 `isinstance(x, type)`, `currentframe` \u2192 `sys._getframe` | ~10ms |\n| `console.py` | Defer `pretty` to `Console.print()` | ~3-5ms |\n| `console.py` | Defer `scope` to `Console.log()` (only when `log_locals=True`) | ~3-5ms |\n| `console.py` | Defer `getpass` to `Console.input()` (only when `password=True`) | ~2ms |\n| `console.py` | Defer `html.escape` and `zlib` to export methods | ~2.3ms |\n| `console.py` | Remove dead `_svg_hash` function (unused since 113997ac, fixes latent NameError) | cleanup |\n| `segment.py` | Remove dead `logging` import (`getLogger` assigned but never used) | ~2-3ms |\n| `theme.py` | Defer `configparser` to `Theme.from_file()` | ~1.5ms |\n| `syntax.py` | Move `Console`, `ConsoleOptions`, `JustifyMethod`, `RenderResult` to `TYPE_CHECKING` | eliminates console.py from syntax import chain |\n| `protocol.py` | Replace `from inspect import isclass` \u2192 `isinstance(x, type)` | prepares for dataclasses removal |\n| `repr.py` | Defer `import inspect` to `auto_rich_repr()` | prepares for dataclasses removal |\n\nAll deferred imports are still available at runtime \u2014 they're loaded when the code path that needs them is actually hit. The `TYPE_CHECKING` imports are annotation-only and never needed at runtime (files use `from __future__ import annotations`).\n\n## Benchmarks\n\nEnvironment: Standard_D2s_v5 (non-burstable), CPython 3.13.13, hyperfine --min-runs 30\n\n### Per-module breakdown (this PR)\n\n| Module | Time |\n|---|---|\n| `import rich` | 18.2ms |\n| `Console` | 52.5ms |\n| `RichHandler` | 56.4ms |\n| `Syntax` | 64.7ms |\n| `Traceback` | 92.5ms |\n| `Markdown` | 103.0ms |\n\n## Motivation\n\npip vendors Rich, and `Console`/`RichHandler` are imported on every `pip` invocation. These savings directly improve startup time for pip and every other tool that imports Rich.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `pytest tests/` \u2014 952 passed, 25 skipped\n- `mypy -p rich --no-incremental` \u2014 no issues\n- `black --check` \u2014 passes\n\n## Disclosure\n\nThis PR was developed with AI assistance (codeflash-agent). Benchmarked in a controlled environment (dedicated non-burstable Azure VM). All tests verified across multiple Python implementations."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in rich/logging.py (hunk at line 217).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"rich/logging.py\", \"old_text\": \"        *,\\n        record: LogRecord,\\n        traceback: Optional[Traceback],\\n        message_renderable: \\\"ConsoleRenderable\\\",\\n    ) -> \\\"ConsoleRenderable\\\":\\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"Render log for display.\\n\\n        Args:\", \"new_text\": \"        *,\\n        record: LogRecord,\\n        traceback: Optional[Traceback],\\n        message_renderable: ConsoleRenderable,\\n    ) -> ConsoleRenderable:\\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"Render log for display.\\n\\n        Args:\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}], "metadata": {"source": "programmatic", "instance_id": "rich_4070", "repo": "Textualize/rich", "base_commit": "fc41075a3206d2a5fd846c6f41c4d2becab814fa", "files": ["rich/_emoji_replace.py", "rich/console.py", "rich/emoji.py", "rich/logging.py", "rich/protocol.py", "rich/repr.py", "rich/segment.py", "rich/syntax.py", "rich/theme.py"], "symbols": ["_emoji_replace", "render", "_collect_renderables", "is_expandable"], "n_hunks": 33, "n_files": 9, "stripped_hunks": 0, "lossy_hunks": 0, "test_target": ["tests/test_console.py", "tests/test_syntax.py"], "test_target_source": "test_patch", "synthesized": {"graph_observations": true, "repro_observations": true, "test_observations": true, "read_observations": "snapshot", "reasoning_text": true, "repro_script": "generic"}, "note": "Programmatic replay of the reference patch; graph/repro/test observations are synthesized from patch context, not live tool outputs. Read-file observations are snapshot.", "turns": 45, "generator": "gen_programmatic.py v1", "traj_turn": 29, "tool": "edit_file", "est_tokens": 1870, "split_from": "rich_4070"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "# Coder \u2014 system prompt\n\nYou are an offline autonomous Python coding agent working in /workspace, a\nrepository snapshot at the commit just before the fix. You resolve one\nissue per session: diagnose the bug or feature request, write the\nsmallest patch that makes the hidden tests pass, and submit it.\n\nThe hidden tests are applied AFTER your patch in a clean container. Test\nfiles and runner config are reset there, so editing them here is wasted\nwork and stripped from your patch. Fix library code only.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- NEVER edit, create, or delete test files or runner config: tests,\n  conftest, pytest.ini, pyproject.toml, tox.ini, setup.cfg, and any\n  packaging or test-runner file. If a fix seems to need a test change,\n  you are wrong: re-read the issue and the library code.\n- NEVER write scratch scripts, repro scripts, or temp files under\n  /workspace: anything left there enters the patch diff. Scratch work\n  goes under /tmp only, and is deleted before submit.\n- Minimal patches only: change the fewest lines that fix the issue,\n  preserve existing style and names, do not refactor, do not add\n  features the issue does not ask for.\n- Never guess an API or symbol: read the code first.\n- Do not browse the network: there is none. All tools are offline.\n\n## Workflow (follow in order, do not skip steps)\n\n1. READ THE TASK. Extract: what fails, the exact error or wrong\n   behaviour, and any failing test names mentioned in the issue.\n2. LOCALIZE (graph first). Call the code_analyzer sub-agent once with\n   the issue text and the failing test names. Then use the graph tools\n   yourself on the SYMBOLS it returns (never natural-language queries):\n   search_similar_code for symbol lookup, get_code_neighbors for\n   callers and callees, get_code_subgraph for the surrounding call\n   graph. Only then read_file the 1-3 files that matter. Skim, do not\n   dump whole files: read_file returns at most 150 lines per call.\n   Use the graph-navigation skill for the exact procedure.\n3. REPRODUCE FIRST. Before editing anything, write a small script\n   under /tmp that triggers the reported failure, and run it with\n   run_command. Confirm you see the reported failure. If you cannot\n   reproduce it, your localization is wrong: go back to step 2. Use\n   the reproduce-first skill for the exact procedure.\n4. FIX. Make one small edit at a time with edit_file (prefer it over\n   write_file, which rewrites whole files). Re-run the /tmp repro\n   after each edit. One hypothesis at a time: if a fix does not change\n   the repro outcome, revert it before trying the next idea.\n5. VERIFY (narrow to wide). Run the narrowest relevant test first\n   (single test, quiet, short traceback), then the containing test\n   file, then the affected package directory. After every pytest run,\n   use the test-summary skill: never paste raw pytest output into\n   your reasoning, condense it to a short failure summary first.\n   Stop expanding the test scope once the repro passes and the\n   directly related tests pass.\n6. CLEAN AND SUBMIT. Delete any scratch files under /workspace (there\n   should be none), run get_status to confirm the diff touches only\n   library code, then call submit_patch. get_status and submit_patch\n   are free and do not count toward the tool-call budget.\n\n## Budget discipline\n\n- You have about 5 minutes and about 50 tool calls per task. Poll\n  get_status every 8-10 calls. If fewer than 10 calls remain, stop\n  exploring and submit the best patch you have.\n- If the same edit fails 3 times, or the same test fails 3 times with\n  no change, stop and submit the best patch instead of looping.\n- Keep the context small: short reads, short repro output, summarized\n  test output. The context ceiling is 32,768 tokens and a flooded\n  context is a failed task."}, {"role": "user", "content": "fix for padding width\n\nFixes https://github.com/Textualize/rich/issues/3871"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in rich/table.py (hunk at line 531).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"rich/table.py\", \"old_text\": \"            self._measure_column(console, options, column) for column in columns\\n        ]\\n        widths = [_range.maximum or 1 for _range in width_ranges]\\n        get_padding_width = self._get_padding_width\\n        extra_width = self._extra_width\\n        if self.expand:\", \"new_text\": \"            self._measure_column(console, options, column) for column in columns\\n        ]\\n        widths = [_range.maximum or 1 for _range in width_ranges]\\n\\n        get_padding_width = self._get_padding_width\\n        extra_width = self._extra_width\\n        if self.expand:\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Edit applied."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in rich/table.py (hunk at line 701).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"rich/table.py\", \"old_text\": \"    def _get_padding_width(self, column_index: int) -> int:\\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"Get extra width from padding.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n        _, pad_right, _, pad_left = self.padding\\n        if self.collapse_padding:\\n            if column_index > 0:\\n                pad_left = max(0, pad_left - pad_right)\\n        return pad_left + pad_right\\n\\n    def _measure_column(\", \"new_text\": \"    def _get_padding_width(self, column_index: int) -> int:\\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"Get extra width from padding.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n        _, pad_right, _, pad_left = self.padding\\n\\n        if self.collapse_padding:\\n            pad_left = 0\\n            pad_right = abs(pad_left - pad_right)\\n\\n        if not self.pad_edge:\\n            if column_index == 0:\\n                pad_left = 0\\n            if column_index == len(self.columns) - 1:\\n                pad_right = 0\\n\\n        return pad_left + pad_right\\n\\n    def _measure_column(\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Edit applied."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in rich/table.py (hunk at line 727).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"rich/table.py\", \"old_text\": \"            return Measurement(0, 0)\\n\\n        padding_width = self._get_padding_width(column._index)\\n\\n        if column.width is not None:\\n            # Fixed width column\\n            return Measurement(\", \"new_text\": \"            return Measurement(0, 0)\\n\\n        padding_width = self._get_padding_width(column._index)\\n        if column.width is not None:\\n            # Fixed width column\\n            return Measurement(\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Edit applied."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Applying the fix in rich/table.py (hunk at line 763).\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"arguments\": {\"path\": \"rich/table.py\", \"old_text\": \"            self._get_cells(console, column_index, column)\\n            for column_index, column in enumerate(self.columns)\\n        )\\n        row_cells: List[Tuple[_Cell, ...]] = list(zip(*_column_cells))\\n        _box = (\\n            self.box.substitute(\", \"new_text\": \"            self._get_cells(console, column_index, column)\\n            for column_index, column in enumerate(self.columns)\\n        )\\n\\n        row_cells: List[Tuple[_Cell, ...]] = list(zip(*_column_cells))\\n        _box = (\\n            self.box.substitute(\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Edit applied."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Re-running the repro after the edit to check the fix.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"run_command\", \"arguments\": {\"command\": \"python /tmp/repro_rich_3935.py\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}, {"role": "user", "content": "PASS: repro script now succeeds (no failure output after the fix)."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Repro passes. Now the narrowest relevant tests.\n<|tool_call|>{\"name\": \"run_command\", \"arguments\": {\"command\": \"python -m pytest tests/test_columns.py tests/test_table.py -q\"}}<|tool_call_end|>"}], "metadata": {"source": "programmatic", "instance_id": "rich_3935", "repo": "Textualize/rich", "base_commit": "fe55a131c2780fa856464ad04d7d6dc8a1079b72", "files": ["rich/table.py"], "symbols": ["__rich_console__", "_calculate_column_widths", "get_padding", "_get_padding_width"], "n_hunks": 5, "n_files": 1, "stripped_hunks": 0, "lossy_hunks": 0, "test_target": ["tests/test_columns.py", "tests/test_table.py"], "test_target_source": "test_patch", "synthesized": {"graph_observations": true, "repro_observations": true, "test_observations": true, "read_observations": "snapshot", "reasoning_text": true, "repro_script": "generic"}, "note": "Programmatic replay of the reference patch; graph/repro/test observations are synthesized from patch context, not live tool outputs. Read-file observations are snapshot.", "turns": 15, "generator": "gen_programmatic.py v1", "traj_turn": 12, "tool": "run_command", "est_tokens": 1849, "split_from": "rich_3935"}}
